WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:01.350
- Battle Rap.

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:03.960
A war of words using intricate schemes,

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.493
raw syncopated delivery,

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.060
and witty wordplay.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:10.740
Lyricists turned the art of rap

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.690
into a clash of the titans.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.120
- Now I'm gonna have the crowd
swinging from these bars.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.680
I ain't with the monkey business.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.291
I like that move. Pocket check back,

00:00:18.291 --> 00:00:19.589
(crowd cheers)
but it's just too bad

00:00:19.589 --> 00:00:21.930
he ain't got no money in it.
(crowd shouts)

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.970
- Over the years, Battle Rap has expanded

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.650
from the hip-hop underground

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.080
and into mainstream pop culture.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.760
Whether it's Eminem's "Eight Mile,"

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.440
Netflix's "Rhythm and Flow,"

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.210
James Corden's "Dropped the Mic"

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.850
or the epic rap battles
of history on YouTube.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.220
Today, Battle Rap is a professional sport

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.430
with superstar athletes and
leagues all around the world.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.110
- And one of the most popular leagues

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:44.269
is the Ultimate Rap League

00:00:44.269 --> 00:00:46.650
or URL for short.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.590
- We all together basically
organized the culture

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.660
and just made a business out of it,

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.460
really making it and turning this whole

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:54.960
art form into a league.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:55.978
- For over a decade,

00:00:55.978 --> 00:01:00.060
the URL has drawn over 650
million views on YouTube

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.580
and provided viral Battle Rap moments.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.251
- Consider it an art form, because

00:01:05.251 --> 00:01:06.571
the fact that

00:01:06.571 --> 00:01:09.060
these guys go up there and put on a show,

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.700
it's all theater, it's
all performing in art.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.259
- So where did Battle Rap come from

00:01:13.259 --> 00:01:17.070
and how did it become so
popular across the world?

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.000
- [Arthur Buckner] This is the story

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.790
of how Battle Rap evolved
from underground competitions

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.140
to the global streaming
phenomenon it is today.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.717
(intense hip-hop instrumental music)

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:35.070
- I've been a Battle
Rap fan for a long time.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.338
For years I've followed
the culture on YouTube

00:01:37.338 --> 00:01:38.850
watching my favorite battlers

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.880
how I watch my favorite
drummers or basketball players.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.139
But I've always wondered how
Battle Rap got its start.

00:01:44.139 --> 00:01:46.320
So let's get into it.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.270
- The art of spoken word has been around

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:49.770
since ancient times.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.990
For centuries, Grios from West Africa

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.400
have recounted tribal history

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.340
to their communities
through music and poetry.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.320
In the West Indies, Calypso artists

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.150
have engaged in Picong Duels,

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:03.201
where singers fire rhyming
insults at each other in song.

00:02:03.201 --> 00:02:06.140
- [Picong Duelist] I will
tell you plain and candidly,

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.197
don't stop look back and smile

00:02:08.197 --> 00:02:11.361
because you have a face like crocodile.

00:02:11.361 --> 00:02:14.684
- In black American culture,
young people played The Dozens.

00:02:14.684 --> 00:02:16.800
A verbal game where people take turns,

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.720
lobbing humorous insults at each other

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.130
in front of an audience.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.110
But Battle Rap as we know it today,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.373
didn't start until the 1980s.

00:02:24.373 --> 00:02:27.475
(upbeat hip-hop instrumental)

00:02:27.475 --> 00:02:28.375
In the early eighties,

00:02:28.375 --> 00:02:30.750
hip-hop collectives attended conventions

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:31.860
to compete against each other

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.990
for prize money and bragging rights.

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:34.823
At first,

00:02:34.823 --> 00:02:36.870
these competitions were friendly face offs

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:38.790
to prove which crew could do a better job

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:39.990
at rocking the crowd.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.970
That was until the
Christmas Rappers Contest

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.612
at the Harlem World Club in 1981.

00:02:44.612 --> 00:02:45.690
That's when a battle

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.080
between Kool Moe Dee and Busy Bee Starski,

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.140
changed how MCs approached
rap competition forever.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:53.640
Busy Bee's claim that he would knock

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.426
out all bums before the contest,

00:02:55.426 --> 00:02:57.900
was not taken well by Kool Moe Dee.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.144
Determined to make a point,

00:02:59.144 --> 00:03:01.140
Kool Moe Dee entered the contest

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.150
after Busy Bee finished his set

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:04.710
and rocked the crowd with a verse

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.652
that directly attacked Busy
Bee's credibility as a rapper.

00:03:07.652 --> 00:03:09.436
♪ Hold on Busy Bee, I
don't mean to be bold, ♪

00:03:09.436 --> 00:03:11.532
♪ but put that "Ba-Ditty-Ba"
bullshit on hold. ♪

00:03:11.532 --> 00:03:13.654
♪ We gonna get right
down to the nitty-grit. ♪

00:03:13.654 --> 00:03:16.470
♪ Gonna tell you a little
something why you ain't shit. ♪

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.090
- Kool Moe Dee's rhyming disses

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.166
inspired other MCs to adopt his style

00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:22.020
and engage in small rap battles

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.870
at basketball courts and
corner stores around New York.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.790
This style helped move the the genre away

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:30.120
from the old school, upbeat,
party themed hip-hop,

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.300
into a grittier art that
showcased lyrical ability.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.500
Roxanne Shante was

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.140
among the first female
Battle Rappers in the 80's,

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.968
and a Rap Beef pioneer
starting the Roxanne War,

00:03:39.968 --> 00:03:44.631
with her infamous disc track,
"Roxanne's Revenge," in 1984.

00:03:44.631 --> 00:03:46.468
♪ My name is Roxanne and I came to say, ♪

00:03:46.468 --> 00:03:48.909
♪ I'm rockin to the beat
and I do it every day. ♪

00:03:48.909 --> 00:03:51.150
- As hip-hop entered the 90's,

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:53.250
the tradition of calling
out your peers on record

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:56.610
continued, influencing
several infamous Rap Beefs.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:58.560
Memorable conflicts between artists like

00:03:58.560 --> 00:03:59.940
Ice Cube and Common,

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:01.407
Dr. Dre and Uncle Luke,

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:02.790
Tupac and Biggie,

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:06.090
and most notably, the exchange
between Jay-Z and Nas,

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.115
have been etched into hip-hop history.

00:04:08.115 --> 00:04:10.505
♪ Went from, Nasty Nas to Esco's Trash ♪

00:04:10.505 --> 00:04:13.986
♪ had a spark when you started,
but now you just garbage. ♪

00:04:13.986 --> 00:04:16.125
♪ Talk about me, laugh behind
my back, but in my face ♪

00:04:16.125 --> 00:04:18.420
y'all some "Well Wishing,"
friendly, acting,

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:19.560
envy, hiding snakes and snakes"

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.140
- Over the next decade,
battling on the streets for fun

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:24.116
grew more popular and became a subculture

00:04:24.116 --> 00:04:27.033
of competing rappers testing their skills.

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:30.060
(mid-tempo hip-hop music)

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.160
In the mid nineties, hip-hop festivals

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.070
like Scribble Jam, featured
freestyle battle competitions,

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:37.353
that would draw up-and-coming
rappers to battle

00:04:37.353 --> 00:04:40.200
for a chance to earn prize money, trophies

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.400
and exposure.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.272
Seeing the appeal of
freestyle competitions,

00:04:43.272 --> 00:04:46.002
TV networks began
incorporating Rap Battles

00:04:46.002 --> 00:04:47.730
into their programming.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:50.250
Shows like HBO's "Blaze Battle,"

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:52.320
MTV's "MC Battle"

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.570
and BET's "106 and Park"

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.423
would bring Battle Rap
to worldwide television.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:01.140
In the early two thousands,
Troy Mitchell, better known

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:03.400
as Smack White, had begun
interviewing and filming

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.928
several established artists like 50 Cent,

00:05:05.928 --> 00:05:07.160
Beanie Siegel,

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:08.400
DMX

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:09.510
and Cameron.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:11.520
But he wasn't just covering celebrities.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.640
Smack would also film with
up-and-coming East coast rappers.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.140
He would record their freestyles

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:17.580
and produce any music videos

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.800
to help promote their image and mix-tapes.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.600
Smack then took all of this footage

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.900
and created a DVD Video
magazine called "Smack DVD".

00:05:26.601 --> 00:05:29.610
- I used to go around,
do exclusive interviews,

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.250
shoot videos, help them
promote they projects,

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:36.210
help them, show them in
environments and places

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:40.500
that you won't normally see
them in, on a regular standard,

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:42.960
TV network, like MTV, BET.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:43.793
- [Speaker] Right?
- [Smack White] Yeah.

00:05:43.793 --> 00:05:45.480
You gonna see them in the studio,

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:46.893
you gonna see 'em in airplanes,

00:05:46.893 --> 00:05:50.160
in they house, on they
block in they neighborhoods.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.922
- Vlogging, before vlogging was a thing.

00:05:51.922 --> 00:05:53.190
That's literally what it was.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:54.949
- Heavily bootlegged, smack DVDs

00:05:54.949 --> 00:05:57.120
made it to outlets all around the world.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:59.040
And became one of the most popular sources

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.740
for uncensored hip-hop news and music.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.088
Smack began including rap battles

00:06:03.088 --> 00:06:04.920
near the end of each volume.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.806
And, in keeping with Battle Rap's origins,

00:06:06.806 --> 00:06:09.930
these battles took place
in gyms, barbershops

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:12.930
and on street corners where
crowds could watch the show.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:16.800
- I closed it off with a
classic hip-hop battle.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:17.633
And,

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:21.660
that segment of the DVD got so popular,

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:25.361
people used to buy the DVD
with mainstream artists like,

00:06:25.361 --> 00:06:27.033
Eminem, 50 Cent,

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.001
Cameron, Fat Joe,

00:06:31.001 --> 00:06:33.270
Buster Rhymes.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:34.812
I had all these major artists

00:06:34.812 --> 00:06:36.510
on the front of the DVD

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.640
with major coverage of these artists.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.190
And people used to
basically go buy the DVD

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:43.380
and go to the back of the DVD first,

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:44.550
to watch the Battles.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:48.108
- Smack. I literally, my first
experience is exactly what

00:06:48.108 --> 00:06:50.017
you described here. I'm interested like

00:06:50.017 --> 00:06:51.990
"What's all this stuff?
What's this? No, no, no, no.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:54.120
Me skipping, skipping, skipping,
this one we gotta see,"

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:57.398
so I never even caught
the promo, the interviews.

00:06:57.398 --> 00:06:59.489
But the battle was the...absolutely.

00:06:59.489 --> 00:07:00.487
(Smack chuckles)

00:07:00.487 --> 00:07:03.164
- Waiting for his chance and
now he feeling time's perfect.

00:07:03.164 --> 00:07:04.525
He probably ain't want a battle

00:07:04.525 --> 00:07:07.230
just wanted to meet me in person.

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:09.200
- Then World Star came into play.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.160
- I ain't think about World Star.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:12.870
- And they made a killing
because they wasn't

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:16.140
into content creation,
but they was into traffic.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:17.340
- Absolutely. Absolutely.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:18.771
- So they killed me.

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:20.853
That business model,

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:24.225
at that time was a success.

00:07:24.225 --> 00:07:28.440
Moving forward to 2000 and 2009,

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:29.523
I just had to figure out,

00:07:29.523 --> 00:07:33.120
what was the most valuable
content that still exists

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:36.090
with what I was producing?
And that was Battle Rap.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:39.090
- As Smack DVD rap battles
grew in popularity,

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:41.400
Smack saw an opportunity
to create a platform

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.430
for Battle Rappers to perform
in front of larger crowds.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:47.880
That's when the Ultimate
Rap League was born.

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:49.691
Better known as the URL,

00:07:49.691 --> 00:07:52.260
The Ultimate Rap League
moved from the streets

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:53.490
and began renting venues

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:56.400
for Battle Rap events to house
their growing number of fans.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.937
By 2009, the URL started
posting their battles

00:07:58.937 --> 00:08:02.400
on YouTube for viewers to watch online.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.674
It was Tay Rock's Seatbelt
Haymaker that shook

00:08:04.674 --> 00:08:07.830
the internet, that solidified the URL

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:10.830
as one of the most exciting
channels to watch on YouTube.

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:13.080
- You get another cut and another cut.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.411
It looked like the seatbelt won't work

00:08:14.411 --> 00:08:17.697
(crowd roars)
and I'm trying to buckle up.

00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:19.283
- And there are more
and more Battle Rappers

00:08:19.283 --> 00:08:21.900
becoming superstars in the community.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:23.040
Like Geechi Gotti.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.442
With 57 battles on YouTube alone

00:08:25.442 --> 00:08:28.260
and over 32 million views,

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:30.960
Geechi Gotti has been
URL's champion of the year

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:32.310
for three years in a row.

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:34.380
As Battle Rap culture grew online,

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.770
more and more leagues started
popping up all over the world.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:40.950
Leagues like the Canadian
owned "King Of The Dot,"

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:43.167
the woman lead, "Queen of the Ring

00:08:43.167 --> 00:08:47.370
and the Filipino "Flip Top"
and the UK "Don't Flop,"

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:49.410
have pulled in millions of subscribers.

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:52.230
Do you consider Battle Rap to be a sport?

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:53.823
- Yes, absolutely.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.310
When I first started representing

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:00.540
the art form of Battle Rap,

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.450
I modeled it after,

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:06.708
I tried to model it after the
UFC, you know what I'm saying?

00:09:06.708 --> 00:09:09.240
Everly influence from the UFC...

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.846
- Y'all don't play, yo hold up. Hold up.

00:09:11.846 --> 00:09:13.800
- Just wanted to come up with a name

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:15.453
that people will always remember.

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:18.960
- So now that you know how
Battle Rap got started,

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:20.280
let's break down how it works.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:22.140
A battle is usually two competitors

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.180
going back and forth for three rounds.

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:26.160
And at the end, it's up to the audience

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:27.630
to decide the winner.

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:29.790
- We saw Mike (indistinct)
- [DJ] Yeah.

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:32.340
- There are several widely
used forms of wordplay

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:36.746
but the most common are
Angles, Schemes, Haymakers

00:09:36.746 --> 00:09:38.640
and Punches.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.409
Punches are short for punchlines.

00:09:40.409 --> 00:09:43.650
That the line's delivered
at the climax of a setup.

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:45.300
- You gang bang what?

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:46.697
I'm supposed to be scared of 'em?

00:09:46.697 --> 00:09:48.690
I will have you and your flag hanging

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:51.118
off a balcony like I'm
proud to be an American.

00:09:51.118 --> 00:09:52.020
(crowd cheers)

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.960
- Extra powerful punches
are called Haymakers.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.000
They're usually accompanied
by a small pause

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.860
to generate a stronger crowd reaction.

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:00.210
- I was up in the hotel,

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:03.000
loading rounds for cousin,
big rounds I'm stuffin,

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:03.833
they say, "ill in the lobby,"

00:10:03.833 --> 00:10:05.550
I grabbed the nose running.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:07.410
I'm coming down with somethin.
- Oh my God.

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:09.300
- For flair and creativity,

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:11.700
A battler may also use a Scheme.

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:14.167
This is when a rapper
presents a subject matter

00:10:14.167 --> 00:10:17.010
and then follows it up
with a long form parallel

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:19.110
comparing the subject
matter to something else.

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:21.090
- Your career is just like Shaq's.

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:22.800
He spent four years in Orlando

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.780
and learnt the harder
way he couldn't hack it.

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:26.610
Smack brought in a certain PG

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:28.380
and then we all believed in magic.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:30.150
You became lost and jealous

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:31.936
ain't find out the way to compete.

00:10:31.936 --> 00:10:34.427
- While a lot of Battle
Rap verses are written,

00:10:34.427 --> 00:10:36.960
there are also freestyles and rebuttals.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:39.390
Freestyles are where you
improvise bars on the spot.

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:40.950
You can reference your opponent's clothes

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:42.600
or anything that's
happening in the moment.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:43.935
A rebuttal is similar

00:10:43.935 --> 00:10:46.530
but instead, you improvise bars responding

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:48.360
to something your opponent just said.

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:50.640
And lastly, we have Angles.

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:52.410
An Angle is a personal attack

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:54.420
that points out your opponent's flaws.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:55.795
Angles are all about focusing

00:10:55.795 --> 00:10:59.190
on specific things about your
opponent to break them down.

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:00.843
- I ain't saying your shit wack,

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:04.080
but bro, it's the same move
every time you kick that.

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:05.640
Set up, punchline, set up, punchline.

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:07.050
That's how you spit that?

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:09.510
Just a candy wrapper with four bars

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:10.343
you a...

00:11:10.343 --> 00:11:11.388
see how easy it is to predict that?

00:11:11.388 --> 00:11:13.530
(crowd roars)

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:16.800
- Best punchlines come from
with what resonates to people.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:18.600
So you don't wanna say something that

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:20.430
a bunch of people don't know about

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:22.821
and then it's like when
you got a audience there,

00:11:22.821 --> 00:11:25.140
they can't pull out their
phone or go to Google

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:26.130
and look it up.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:27.067
So it's just about,

00:11:27.067 --> 00:11:30.300
reading the room, knowing your audience.

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:33.060
On the URL stage I'm gonna
get a little more intricate

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:34.907
because they already understand exactly

00:11:34.907 --> 00:11:36.750
what it is that we do.

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:39.540
- What are the differences between

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:43.230
a Battle Rapper and a Studio Rapper?

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:45.060
Or what, what are some
common misconceptions?

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:48.213
- Common misconception is
Battle Rappers can't make music.

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:52.470
They, put us in this very, very small box.

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:53.393
You know what I'm saying?

00:11:53.393 --> 00:11:56.190
It's a bad stigma they got on us.

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:57.360
They say we can make music.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:11:59.310
I just don't understand
because Battle Rappers

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:01.230
are clearly the best writers,

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:03.690
as far as element of being an MC.

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:05.040
'Cause you only get one opportunity.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:06.630
You can never say this again.

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:07.590
You have one take.

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:09.270
You have to wow a audience,

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:11.520
time and time again with new materials.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:14.939
- In my whole career,
everything I did was a throwaway

00:12:14.939 --> 00:12:16.620
'cause I can never use...

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:18.600
If I use those bars again, I'm wrong.

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:19.533
- You recycle it.

00:12:19.533 --> 00:12:22.950
- I'm wrong for using my own lyrics again.

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:25.440
So our job is much harder.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:27.630
- Even if they're not
creating chart toppers,

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:29.790
Battle Rappers still have a huge influence

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:31.470
on popular culture today.

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:33.390
Whether it's ESPN commercials,

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:35.280
the BET Hip-Hop Awards

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.200
or reality tv.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:38.590
Then there's shows like Nick Cannon's

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:39.801
"Wild 'N Out"

00:12:39.801 --> 00:12:42.384
which has taken inspiration
from Battle Rap culture

00:12:42.384 --> 00:12:45.501
and even features URL stars Conceited,

00:12:45.501 --> 00:12:47.132
Charlie Clips

00:12:47.132 --> 00:12:48.543
and Hitman Holla.

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:51.360
What kind of things do you
see coming in the future

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:52.290
for Battle Rap?

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:55.470
- I'm trying to make
this a household sport.

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:57.690
What we have accomplished so far,

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:00.090
is amazing because,

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:03.300
if you pay attention to what's
going on in this culture,

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:07.410
you see so many different
territories have adopted,

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:10.983
the style of this art form.

00:13:12.111 --> 00:13:12.990
- It's an art, right?
- It's an art.

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:14.340
- It's entertainment.

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:16.818
And these guys has found a way

00:13:16.818 --> 00:13:20.328
to provide for they families
to entertain us this way.

00:13:20.328 --> 00:13:22.020
And they put on a show for us.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:23.370
And that's what it's about.

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:25.260
- Even though it started
in the underground,

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:27.990
the sport has become so
popular that teenagers who once

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:30.120
dreamed of being the next big rap artist,

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:33.570
are now dreaming of being the
next great Battle Rap star.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:35.497
- Before you go, I
wanna let you know about

00:13:35.497 --> 00:13:37.720
"Fight the Power How
Hip-Hop changed the World,"

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:41.820
a new PBS series hosted
by hip-hop legend Chuck D.

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:43.960
It's about how hip-hop
became a global movement

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:46.230
that spoke truth to power.

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:48.360
Check out the link in
the description below

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:50.553
